  <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
  <h1>Import an existing budget</h1>
  <p class="lede">Moving from another budgeting app? ZxBudget can import that budget. Save the export as two CSV files in the YNAB export format: a register and a plan.</p>
  <p>In Settings, and in the File menu on Mac, choose <strong>Import from YNAB</strong>. You give it a Register CSV and a Plan CSV.</p>

  <h2>Register</h2>
  <p>The register is one row for each transaction. The first line names the columns.</p>
  <pre class="csv"><code>Account,Flag,Date,Payee,Category Group/Category,Category Group,Category,Memo,Outflow,Inflow,Cleared</code></pre>
  <p>ZxBudget treats the file as a register when that header includes <strong>Account</strong> and <strong>Cleared</strong>. A blank column stays in the row, so the commas stay too.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Account</strong> is the account’s name.</li>
    <li><strong>Flag</strong> is Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, or Purple, a name of your own, or blank. During import you match any name that is not already one of those colors.</li>
    <li><strong>Date</strong> is the day, written <strong>2019-04-03</strong>. A date after today comes in as a one-time upcoming transaction.</li>
    <li><strong>Payee</strong> is who was paid, or who paid you. A transfer is written <strong>Transfer :</strong> plus the other account’s name, and each account has its own row. <strong>Starting Balance</strong> is the opening amount: Inflow when the account holds money, Outflow when the amount is owed. <strong>Reconciliation Balance Adjustment</strong> is a difference found while reconciling.</li>
    <li><strong>Category Group/Category</strong> repeats the group and category as <strong>Everyday: Groceries</strong>. ZxBudget reads the next two columns instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Category Group</strong> and <strong>Category</strong> are where the money is categorized. Income uses the group <strong>Inflow</strong> and the category <strong>Ready to Assign</strong>. A tracking account can leave Category empty. A name can begin with an emoji, which ZxBudget keeps as the icon.</li>
    <li><strong>Memo</strong> is a note. A split is two or more rows with the same date and account. The memo starts with <strong>Split (1/2)</strong>, <strong>Split (2/2)</strong>, and so on. The payee on each of those rows can differ.</li>
    <li><strong>Outflow</strong> and <strong>Inflow</strong> are the amount, written <strong>$41.15</strong>. The side that was not used is <strong>$0.00</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Cleared</strong> is <strong>Uncleared</strong>, <strong>Cleared</strong>, or <strong>Reconciled</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A register in this shape looks like this. These rows are not from a real budget.</p>
  <pre class="csv"><code>Account,Flag,Date,Payee,Category Group/Category,Category Group,Category,Memo,Outflow,Inflow,Cleared
Chequing,,2019-04-03,Market Day,Everyday: Groceries,Everyday,Groceries,,$41.15,$0.00,Reconciled
Chequing,,2019-04-07,Northwind,Inflow: Ready to Assign,Inflow,Ready to Assign,,$0.00,$880.00,Reconciled
Cash Card,,2019-04-11,Weekday Fare,Everyday: Transport,Everyday,Transport,,$8.05,$0.00,Cleared
Cash Card,Yellow,2019-04-16,Lunch Counter,Everyday: Dining Out,Everyday,Dining Out,,$5.55,$0.00,Uncleared
Chequing,,2019-04-21,Hardware Store,Household: Repairs,Household,Repairs,Split (1/2) Paint,$64.10,$0.00,Reconciled
Chequing,,2019-04-21,Paper Goods,Household: Supplies,Household,Supplies,Split (2/2) Paper,$33.33,$0.00,Reconciled
Chequing,,2019-02-02,Transfer : Rainy Day,,,,,$128.40,$0.00,Reconciled
Rainy Day,,2019-02-02,Transfer : Chequing,,,,,$0.00,$128.40,Reconciled
Chequing,,2019-02-02,Starting Balance,,,,,$0.00,$440.00,Reconciled
Cash Card,,2019-02-02,Starting Balance,,,,,$33.33,$0.00,Reconciled
Chequing,,2019-04-03,Reconciliation Balance Adjustment,,,,,$0.00,$41.15,Reconciled
Cash Card,,2027-04-04,Studio Rent,Household: Shelter,Household,Shelter,,$75.25,$0.00,Uncleared</code></pre>

  <h2>Plan</h2>
  <p>The plan is what was assigned to each category in each month. The first line names the columns.</p>
  <pre class="csv"><code>Month,Category Group/Category,Category Group,Category,Assigned,Activity,Available</code></pre>
  <p>ZxBudget treats the file as a plan when that header includes <strong>Month</strong> and <strong>Assigned</strong>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Month</strong> is written <strong>Mar 2019</strong>, or with the full month name, <strong>March 2019</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Category Group/Category</strong> repeats the group and category, the same way as in the register.</li>
    <li><strong>Category Group</strong> and <strong>Category</strong> name the category that received the assignment. A category under <strong>Credit Card Payments</strong> with the same name as an account marks that account as a credit card. A category under <strong>Hidden Categories</strong> with the same name as an account marks that credit card closed.</li>
    <li><strong>Assigned</strong> is the amount for that month, such as <strong>$880.00</strong>, or <strong>-$22.00</strong> when money was moved out of the category. A <strong>$0.00</strong> row is left out.</li>
    <li><strong>Activity</strong> and <strong>Available</strong> may be in the file. ZxBudget does not use them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A plan in this shape looks like this. These rows are not from a real budget.</p>
  <pre class="csv"><code>Month,Category Group/Category,Category Group,Category,Assigned,Activity,Available
Mar 2019,Everyday: Groceries,Everyday,Groceries,$880.00,,
Mar 2019,Everyday: Dining Out,Everyday,Dining Out,-$22.00,,
Mar 2019,Household: Paper Goods,Household,Paper Goods,$0.00,,
Mar 2019,Credit Card Payments: Cash Card,Credit Card Payments,Cash Card,$128.40,,</code></pre>
